{
  "problems": [
    {
      "label": "1",
      "number": "1",
      "statement": "A cyclist travels $7.5$ km in $25$ minutes at constant speed. Find the speed in metres per second.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [],
      "parts_relation": "none"
    },
    {
      "label": "2",
      "number": "2",
      "statement": "A survey records daily reading times of $2$, $4$, $4$, and $10$ minutes.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "(a)",
          "statement": "Calculate the mean time, with units."
        },
        {
          "label": "(b)",
          "statement": "Calculate the median, with units."
        },
        {
          "label": "(c)",
          "statement": "Which measure better describes a typical observation here and why?"
        }
      ],
      "parts_relation": "one_solution"
    },
    {
      "label": "3",
      "number": "3",
      "statement": "In a controlled plant experiment, only light exposure changes; soil, water, species and age are held constant. Explain why controlling those variables helps test whether light affects growth. Does a taller plant in the light group alone prove that light caused growth? Explain.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [],
      "parts_relation": "none"
    },
    {
      "label": "4",
      "number": "4",
      "statement": "A pump fills $1.8$ litres in $45$ seconds at a steady rate. Express its flow in millilitres per second and litres per minute.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [],
      "parts_relation": "none"
    },
    {
      "label": "5",
      "number": "5",
      "statement": "A box has mass $12$ kg. What is its weight? State any assumption needed; distinguish mass from weight.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [],
      "parts_relation": "none"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Convert distance to metres",
      "content": "The distance is $7.5\\text{ km}$. Since $1\\text{ km} = 1000\\text{ m}$,\n$$7.5\\text{ km} = 7.5 \\times 1000 = 7500\\text{ m}.$$",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Convert time to seconds",
      "content": "The time is $25\\text{ min}$. Since $1\\text{ min} = 60\\text{ s}$,\n$$25\\text{ min} = 25 \\times 60 = 1500\\text{ s}.$$",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Compute speed",
      "content": "Speed is distance divided by time:\n$$v = \\frac{7500\\text{ m}}{1500\\text{ s}} = 5\\text{ m/s}.$$",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "The speed is $5\\text{ m/s}$."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Part (a) Mean time",
      "content": "Using general knowledge, the mean is the sum of the observations divided by the number of observations.\n$$\\bar{x}=\\frac{2+4+4+10}{4}=\\frac{20}{4}=5$$\nSo the mean reading time is $5$ minutes.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Part (b) Median time",
      "content": "Order the data: $2, 4, 4, 10$. Since there are $4$ observations, the median is the average of the two middle values.\n$$\\text{median}=\\frac{4+4}{2}=4$$\nSo the median reading time is $4$ minutes.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Part (c) Better typical measure",
      "content": "The median better describes a typical observation here. The value $10$ minutes is much larger than the other times and pulls the mean upward to $5$ minutes, while the median remains $4$ minutes, which is less affected by that high value.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Mean: $5$ minutes. Median: $4$ minutes. The median better describes a typical observation because the $10$-minute value is an outlier that skews the mean upward."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Identify the experimental variables",
      "content": "The variable being tested is light exposure, so it is the independent variable. Plant growth is the outcome being measured, so it is the dependent variable. Soil, water, species, and age are held constant, so they are controlled variables.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Explain why controlling variables helps",
      "content": "Controlling soil, water, species, and age helps because those factors can also affect plant growth. If they are kept the same for all plants, then a difference in growth between the light and no-light groups is less likely to be caused by those other factors. This makes it easier to test whether light itself affects growth.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluate whether one taller plant proves causation",
      "content": "A single taller plant in the light group does not prove that light caused the growth. That plant may have been taller because of random variation, a measurement error, an uncontrolled factor, or simply chance. One observation is not enough to establish a reliable cause-and-effect relationship.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "State what would be needed for stronger evidence",
      "content": "To show that light affects growth, the experiment should use many plants in each group, assign plants randomly, keep all other conditions the same, and compare the average growth of the groups. If the light group consistently grows more than the control group, the evidence for a causal effect becomes stronger.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Controlling soil, water, species, and age helps because it reduces other possible causes of growth differences, making light the main factor being tested. However, a single taller plant in the light group does not prove that light caused the growth; it could be due to chance, individual variation, or an uncontrolled factor. A replicated experiment with a control group and comparison of average growth would be needed for stronger evidence."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Convert litres to millilitres and find millilitres per second",
      "content": "No course context was provided, so this step uses general unit conversion. Since $1\\text{ L}=1000\\text{ mL}$, the volume is $1.8\\text{ L}=1800\\text{ mL}$. The steady flow rate is\n$$\\frac{1800\\text{ mL}}{45\\text{ s}}=40\\text{ mL/s}.$$",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Convert seconds to minutes and find litres per minute",
      "content": "Using $60\\text{ s}=1\\text{ min}$, convert the rate from litres per second to litres per minute:\n$$\\frac{1.8\\text{ L}}{45\\text{ s}}\\times\\frac{60\\text{ s}}{1\\text{ min}}=2.4\\text{ L/min}.$$",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "The flow is $40\\text{ mL/s}$ and $2.4\\text{ L/min}$."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Identify given mass and target quantity",
      "content": "The box has mass $m = 12\\ \\mathrm{kg}$. The question asks for its weight, which is the gravitational force on the box.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "State assumption and weight formula",
      "content": "Using general knowledge, assume the box is near Earth's surface where the gravitational field strength is $g = 9.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$, equivalently $9.8\\ \\mathrm{N/kg}$. Then weight is $W = mg$.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Compute weight",
      "content": "$W = mg = (12\\ \\mathrm{kg})(9.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}) = 117.6\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 118\\ \\mathrm{N}$, directed downward.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Distinguish mass from weight",
      "content": "Mass is a scalar measure of inertia, measured in kilograms, and does not depend on location. Weight is a force due to gravity, measured in newtons, and depends on the local value of $g$.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Assuming $g = 9.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$ near Earth's surface, the weight is $W = mg = (12\\ \\mathrm{kg})(9.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}) = 117.6\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 118\\ \\mathrm{N}$. The mass is $12\\ \\mathrm{kg}$; the weight is the gravitational force, in newtons."
}

